[description]

[b]Azure Boards[/b] tracks work items independently from Git, then links them via IDs, mentions, and branch policies. A [i]process template[/i] (Agile, Scrum, CMMI, or custom) defines work item types and transitions—changing templates later is painful, so choose deliberately at project creation.

[b]Backlogs and boards:[/b] product backlogs order epics/features; sprint boards track iteration scope. Dragging cards updates state machines—ensure WIP limits and definitions of done are social agreements, not just UI columns.

[b]Traceability:[/b] linking builds and commits to work items helps regulated industries; it also adds friction if developers must juggle IDs. Balance with templates and branch naming guidance.

[b]Queries:[/b] WIQL (Work Item Query Language) powers saved queries and dashboards—treat complex queries like code: review and test them.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] Boards describe intent; Git describes reality—use links and policies to keep them honest without drowning engineers in paperwork.
